# [DD-13b.W1.3, Q-W4 — Frank 2026-09-03] A DEFINITION THAT DECLARES AN
# ENCODING THE ARTIFACT IS NOT BUILT FOR.
#
# A definition does not inherit the target's config, so its own `encoding`
# line is a thing it STATED. But D58 makes encoding a per-PATTERN scalar and
# a composed artifact has exactly ONE, so a definition asking for a different
# one is asking for something the format cannot give.
#
# REFUSED, not ignored. A silently dropped directive is a population nobody
# counts, and the author who wrote it has no way to learn it did nothing.
# Equal or absent is fine — `plain` below states the encoding the artifact IS
# built for and composes without complaint, which is what stops the refusal
# from being "any encoding line on a definition".
target = ok
target = clash

pattern ^(?&plain)$
name ok
m "abc" 0 3

pattern ^(?&other)$
name clash

pattern [a-z]+
name plain
encoding byte

pattern [a-z]+
name other
encoding utf8
